From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hendra Manudinata <manoedinata@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 08:02:31 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 042/104] clk: samsung: exynos7570: add the AP-owned USB PLL

The 20 MHz reference clock of the USB PHY comes from a PLL in the WLBT block.
Without the WLBT firmware the AP owns it: select the 26 MHz reference, enable
it, give the ownership to the AP and start the AFC (USBPLL_CON1 in CMU_FSYS),
with the 20 us delay the downstream kernel uses.

Register it as a hand-written clock that feeds the PHY core clock gate, and
give the fixed 60 MHz PHY clock an ID so the devicetree can select it as the
parent of the PHY user mux.

Signed-off-by: Hendra Manudinata <manoedinata@gmail.com>
(cherry picked from commit 5e8110f844e6e37d1d3c6b6244877c763728d0b5)
---
 drivers/clk/samsung/clk-exynos7570.c          | 119 ++++++++++++++++--
 .../clock/samsung,exynos7570-cmu.h            | 112 +++++++++--------
 2 files changed, 169 insertions(+), 62 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/clk/samsung/clk-exynos7570.c b/drivers/clk/samsung/clk-exynos7570.c
index 9ef59e689..59129ca2e 100644
--- a/drivers/clk/samsung/clk-exynos7570.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/samsung/clk-exynos7570.c
@@ -8,14 +8,18 @@
  * "pwrcal" tables for S5E7570. Muxes are modelled by their select field only;
  * the per-mux enable bit (bit 21) is left as the bootloader set it.
  *
- * The USB20 PHY core clock comes from a handshake-controlled 20 MHz USB PLL
- * (WPLL); only its rate is represented here (frat_fsys_wifi2ap_usbpll), its
- * enable sequencing is not modelled yet.
+ * The USB20 PHY core clock comes from the 20 MHz USB PLL (WPLL), which sits
+ * behind the WLBT block. It is registered by hand below and enabled by the AP,
+ * the way the downstream kernel does when WLBT firmware is not using it.
  */
 
 #include <linux/clk-provider.h>
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
+#include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
